  "recipe": {
    "technique": "Making the basic dorodango is a traditional pastime for school children. More recently, the process has been refined into the art of the hikaru (\"shining\") dorodango (光る泥だんご), which has a glossy surface. Several different techniques can be used. Across all methods, a core of the ball is made of basic mud, which has been carefully shaped by hand to be as round as possible. This core is left to dry, and then methodically and carefully dusted with finely sifted soil to create a crust several millimeters thick around the core. This step may be repeated several times, with finer and finer grains of dirt in order to create a smooth and shiny surface."
  },
